The attached image displays the zeta potential of Nano-11 nanoparticles across a pH range from 3 to 11 (Fig. 2C). The zeta potential values consistently remained positive, ranging from approximately +7 mV to +15 mV. As the pH decreased, the zeta potential increased, which is attributed to the protonation of carboxylate groups. This positive surface charge of Nano-11 is crucial for its convenient dispersion in aqueous systems and facilitates the electrostatic adsorption of negatively charged protein antigens like ovalbumin, as further illustrated in panel D and discussed in the text.